      "problem": "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(4a,-2b)$ and $(-2a,2b)$ is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(4a,-2b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2a,2b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4a+(-2a)}{2},\\frac{-2b+2b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2a}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(a,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
